1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,390
Every couple of years, the schools in this
valley do something unusual.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:07,220
Thousands of kids leave their
classrooms and walk,

3
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,230
some of them up to two miles, uphill.

4
00:00:10,118 --> 00:00:14,228
They're practicing for the day Mount Rainier
sends a river of mud down the valley.

5
00:00:14,518 --> 00:00:18,208
In the town of Orting, after the alarm, they
would have roughly forty minutes.

6
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:20,200
And here's the strange part.

7
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:21,820
The mud doesn't need an eruption.

8
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:24,340
The last time a big one came,
there wasn't one.

9
00:00:24,966 --> 00:00:28,606
So how do you live under one of the most
dangerous volcanoes in America?

10
00:00:28,646 --> 00:00:30,846
And what killed the forest that's buried,

11
00:00:30,886 --> 00:00:32,636
standing up, under this town?

12
00:00:34,927 --> 00:00:38,797
Mount Rainier is fourteen thousand four
hundred and ten feet tall,

13
00:00:38,927 --> 00:00:40,857
the highest peak in the Cascades.

14
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:45,357
And it carries more glacier ice than any other
mountain in the lower forty-eight states.

15
00:00:46,324 --> 00:00:48,234
But the real danger is inside.

16
00:00:48,564 --> 00:00:53,024
For thousands of years, hot, acidic water from
the volcano has been eating its rock,

17
00:00:53,064 --> 00:00:55,314
turning parts of it into soft, wet clay.

18
00:00:55,844 --> 00:00:59,234
Surveys from helicopters found the worst
of it high on the west side.

19
00:01:00,109 --> 00:01:02,889
Put ice, water and rotten rock
on a steep mountain,

20
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:04,959
and one day a piece of it lets go.

21
00:01:05,209 --> 00:01:08,879
It mixes with water and becomes a
lahar: a fast river of mud,

22
00:01:09,069 --> 00:01:11,439
boulders and trees, as thick
as wet concrete.

23
00:01:12,367 --> 00:01:17,137
On the steep upper slopes, the biggest lahars can
move faster than a hundred miles an hour.

24
00:01:17,427 --> 00:01:20,117
Down in the valleys, they slow to
fifteen or twenty.

25
00:01:20,427 --> 00:01:22,217
Still faster than anyone can run.

26
00:01:23,076 --> 00:01:25,426
Lava and ash are not the main threat here.

27
00:01:25,736 --> 00:01:28,666
Ash usually blows east, away from
Seattle and Tacoma.

28
00:01:28,976 --> 00:01:31,046
The killer that travels far is the mud.

29
00:01:31,875 --> 00:01:34,905
And the eruption people talk about,
in eighteen ninety-four?

30
00:01:35,155 --> 00:01:37,325
Seattle papers reported black smoke.

31
00:01:37,575 --> 00:01:41,085
A rival paper said it never happened, and
no ash has ever been found.

32
00:01:41,475 --> 00:01:44,965
The last eruption of fresh magma was
about a thousand years ago.

33
00:01:47,811 --> 00:01:50,941
We know how far it can go, because
it has already done it.

34
00:01:51,051 --> 00:01:54,341
About fifty-six hundred years ago, the
top of Rainier collapsed.

35
00:01:54,731 --> 00:01:57,801
The wet, rotten rock became a
gigantic river of mud:

36
00:01:58,011 --> 00:01:59,241
the Osceola Mudflow.

37
00:02:00,127 --> 00:02:04,857
It filled valleys more than three hundred feet deep
and ran for over seventy-five miles.

38
00:02:05,067 --> 00:02:06,477
It even pushed back the sea.

39
00:02:06,847 --> 00:02:10,877
The valley where Kent and Auburn stand today
was once an arm of Puget Sound.

40
00:02:11,107 --> 00:02:12,217
The mud helped fill it.

41
00:02:12,992 --> 00:02:14,182
And it wasn't a one-off.

42
00:02:14,432 --> 00:02:19,022
At least eleven big lahars have reached these
lowlands in the last six thousand years.

43
00:02:19,372 --> 00:02:21,302
In two thousand two, the U.S.

44
00:02:21,572 --> 00:02:25,292
Geological Survey put the chance of another one
in an average lifetime at one in

45
00:02:25,332 --> 00:02:26,382
seven, or more.

46
00:02:27,246 --> 00:02:30,566
Around ninety thousand people now live
in the mapped lahar zones,

47
00:02:30,606 --> 00:02:33,336
in towns like Puyallup, Sumner and Orting,

48
00:02:33,446 --> 00:02:35,676
and more than fifty thousand
people work there.

49
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:38,690
Puyallup has the most people at risk.

50
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:43,230
But Orting has it worst: most of its nine
thousand people live inside the zone.

51
00:02:46,045 --> 00:02:50,665
In nineteen ninety-three, a geologist named Pat
Pringle spotted something strange at

52
00:02:50,705 --> 00:02:53,145
a construction site in
Orting: tree stumps,

53
00:02:53,185 --> 00:02:55,615
still standing upright,
buried in the ground.

54
00:02:56,577 --> 00:02:59,967
Since then, more have turned up, whenever
the town digs for new houses,

55
00:03:00,077 --> 00:03:01,827
a gas station or a school.

56
00:03:02,317 --> 00:03:04,667
Some are Douglas firs up to ten feet wide,

57
00:03:04,737 --> 00:03:06,047
buried right where they stood.

58
00:03:06,939 --> 00:03:09,029
They were killed by the Electron Mudflow,

59
00:03:09,099 --> 00:03:12,489
the largest lahar from Rainier in
the last thousand years.

60
00:03:12,719 --> 00:03:15,949
Where it left the mountains, it was
close to a hundred feet deep.

61
00:03:16,099 --> 00:03:19,689
By Orting, it still buried these trees
up to fifteen feet down.

62
00:03:20,572 --> 00:03:23,122
For years, nobody knew exactly
when it happened.

63
00:03:23,412 --> 00:03:27,062
Then scientists matched the growth rings
of twenty-one buried trees.

64
00:03:27,432 --> 00:03:30,232
Four still had their bark, and all
four died in the same year,

65
00:03:30,272 --> 00:03:31,202
in late summer.

66
00:03:31,532 --> 00:03:33,042
The year was fifteen-oh-seven.

67
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:34,850
And here's what matters.

68
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:37,860
As far as scientists can tell,
there was no eruption.

69
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:41,490
The Electron Mudflow started as a
landslide on the west side,

70
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:44,780
the side with the rotten rock, and
nobody knows what set it off.

71
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:49,120
The Puyallup Tribe has old stories of a flood
of debris that buried this valley,

72
00:03:49,190 --> 00:03:49,980
likely this one.

73
00:03:52,834 --> 00:03:57,024
In nineteen eighty-five, in Colombia, a small eruption
melted ice on the Nevado del Ruiz volcano.

74
00:03:57,594 --> 00:03:59,874
About two hours later, lahars
reached the town of Armero,

75
00:03:59,914 --> 00:04:02,244
built on old mudflow deposits,
just like these towns.

76
00:04:02,454 --> 00:04:04,424
About twenty-three thousand of
its people died.

77
00:04:04,734 --> 00:04:07,904
A hazard map showing Armero in danger had
come out about a month before.

78
00:04:08,174 --> 00:04:10,904
But on the night, nobody could get the
warning to the town in time.

79
00:04:11,181 --> 00:04:13,311
So in nineteen ninety-eight, the U.S.

80
00:04:13,461 --> 00:04:18,091
Geological Survey and Pierce County built a lahar
detection system along these valleys.

81
00:04:18,948 --> 00:04:21,578
It started with tripwires and
sensors along the rivers.

82
00:04:22,068 --> 00:04:25,978
Since twenty sixteen, it has been rebuilt with
twenty-five new seismic stations,

83
00:04:26,108 --> 00:04:28,448
many of them listening for the
low rumble of a flow,

84
00:04:28,488 --> 00:04:30,358
sending data in near real time.

85
00:04:30,628 --> 00:04:33,228
Their data reach emergency centers
in about ten seconds,

86
00:04:33,268 --> 00:04:35,558
and two warning points are
staffed day and night.

87
00:04:35,795 --> 00:04:39,485
When a big lahar is detected, sirens
sound in the valley towns.

88
00:04:39,715 --> 00:04:41,795
For most of them, that means at
least forty minutes,

89
00:04:41,835 --> 00:04:43,745
and up to three hours, of warning.

90
00:04:44,175 --> 00:04:45,645
Orting is near the short end.

91
00:04:46,531 --> 00:04:48,331
A new map from the U.S.

92
00:04:48,371 --> 00:04:50,641
Geological Survey times the worst case.

93
00:04:51,111 --> 00:04:55,321
The mud reaches Orting in about an hour,
Puyallup and Sumner in about three,

94
00:04:55,451 --> 00:04:57,741
and the Tacoma tideflats in four to five.

95
00:04:58,702 --> 00:05:01,052
That hour starts when the
mountain lets go.

96
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:04,532
Sensors need a few minutes to catch it,
and the alarm takes time.

97
00:05:04,762 --> 00:05:06,972
That's why Orting is left
with about forty.

98
00:05:08,017 --> 00:05:11,027
Forget the thirty-foot wall of mud
you may have heard about.

99
00:05:11,257 --> 00:05:14,847
In the model, the front reaching Orting
is about thirteen feet deep,

100
00:05:14,897 --> 00:05:16,807
moving about nine miles an hour.

101
00:05:16,917 --> 00:05:18,307
Still enough to take a house.

102
00:05:19,164 --> 00:05:20,274
The rule is simple.

103
00:05:20,384 --> 00:05:23,854
Walk, don't drive, and get at least a
hundred feet above the valley floor.

104
00:05:24,384 --> 00:05:26,674
Roads would jam, and the mud
follows the valleys.

105
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:29,130
That's why the kids practice.

106
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:33,740
In twenty twenty-four, more than forty-five
thousand students and staff took part in

107
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:35,810
the largest lahar drill in the world.

108
00:05:36,637 --> 00:05:38,047
It's harder than it sounds.

109
00:05:38,337 --> 00:05:42,057
In a drill in two thousand six, the youngest
children in Orting needed about

110
00:05:42,097 --> 00:05:44,887
twenty-five minutes just to reach
the halfway point.

111
00:05:45,777 --> 00:05:49,237
So for about three decades, residents pushed for
a footbridge over the highway that

112
00:05:49,277 --> 00:05:52,907
stands between them and the hills, so kids
wouldn't have to wait at traffic lights.

113
00:05:53,157 --> 00:05:54,527
They call it the Bridge for Kids.

114
00:05:55,157 --> 00:05:57,297
Construction finally began in
January twenty twenty-five,

115
00:05:57,337 --> 00:05:59,377
for about nine million dollars.

116
00:05:59,417 --> 00:06:01,907
A second bridge, over the river,
still has no money.

117
00:06:02,229 --> 00:06:04,319
Even the sirens hold surprises.

118
00:06:04,789 --> 00:06:10,119
In May twenty twenty-three, an old, disconnected siren
in downtown Puyallup went off by mistake.

119
00:06:10,489 --> 00:06:13,139
The county had to post a message:
there is no lahar.

120
00:06:14,062 --> 00:06:16,242
At noon on the first Monday
of every month,

121
00:06:16,282 --> 00:06:18,632
the sirens play a chime, and a voice says:

122
00:06:18,802 --> 00:06:19,692
this is a test.

123
00:06:19,962 --> 00:06:22,152
The real alarm sounds nothing like it.

124
00:06:24,859 --> 00:06:27,449
And there's one scenario nobody
has worked out yet.

125
00:06:27,925 --> 00:06:28,935
In the U.S.

126
00:06:29,325 --> 00:06:34,145
Geological Survey's worst-case model, a giant
landslide lahar reaches Alder Lake in

127
00:06:34,185 --> 00:06:38,955
about fifty minutes, and pushes out enough water to pour
over the three-hundred-and-thirty-foot dam.

128
00:06:39,265 --> 00:06:41,575
What happens after that, the
model doesn't say.

129
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:44,850
And a big lahar could move the
rivers themselves.

130
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:46,050
It's happened before.

131
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:51,200
In nineteen-oh-six, a logjam sent the White River
away from Seattle's side and south

132
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,010
to Tacoma, and it stayed there.

133
00:06:53,835 --> 00:06:55,805
Meanwhile, the mountain is changing.

134
00:06:56,155 --> 00:06:59,605
Since eighteen ninety-six, its glaciers
have lost about half their ice.

135
00:06:59,975 --> 00:07:02,505
One glacier was removed from
the official list.

136
00:07:03,315 --> 00:07:06,965
As the ice melts, it leaves loose rock
and sudden floods behind.

137
00:07:07,435 --> 00:07:11,955
One creek, Tahoma Creek, has had about
thirty-five debris flows and outburst floods

138
00:07:11,995 --> 00:07:13,285
since nineteen sixty-seven.

139
00:07:13,935 --> 00:07:17,265
The road along it has been closed to cars
since the nineteen eighties.

140
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:22,060
And in July twenty twenty-five, Rainier had
the biggest earthquake swarm ever

141
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:26,830
recorded there: about thirteen hundred and fifty
small quakes in about seven weeks.

142
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:30,390
Scientists say it was hot fluids
moving, not magma.

143
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:32,190
The alert level never left normal.

144
00:07:35,096 --> 00:07:37,106
So how do you live under Mount Rainier?

145
00:07:37,256 --> 00:07:38,886
You listen to the mountain day and night,

146
00:07:38,936 --> 00:07:43,186
you teach your kids to walk uphill, and you build
your town knowing what's underneath it.

147
00:07:43,964 --> 00:07:45,674
Which place should we explain next?

148
00:07:45,744 --> 00:07:46,574
Tell me in the comments.

149
00:07:47,175 --> 00:07:51,085
In Orting, the trees from fifteen-oh-seven are
still standing under the streets,

150
00:07:51,235 --> 00:07:52,765
right where the mud caught them.

151
00:07:52,915 --> 00:07:55,615
And every school drill is a
promise that next time,

152
00:07:55,655 --> 00:07:57,605
the people will be gone before it arrives.

153
00:07:58,055 --> 00:07:59,285
This was How It's Run.
